", "watch": "Early general-election polling in South Carolina, or the emergence of nominees with unusual regional strength or weakness, could move the margin several points in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "South Carolina remains a Republican-leaning state in presidential contests, with a partisan lean of about eight points toward the GOP. The 2028 race is an open contest, since the incumbent president is term-limited, so neither party starts with an incumbency advantage. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available yet, leaving state fundamentals as the main guide.\n\nA Republican margin of roughly eight points is the expectation, with wide uncertainty given how little data exists this early. Attention so far has centered on the primaries: both parties are still sorting out nominees, and South Carolina has been chosen to host the first Democratic primary. The eventual nominees' appeal in the South will do more to shape the general election than anything currently measurable.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1807, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2319, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2236, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2334, "margin": -8.0}]}